upd(46,literature_fate='Printed EGAIV4 (1967),20.1.3–4 pp227–228, really uses the unqualified nonzerodivisor/stalk identification;20.1.8 repeats it. Lê’s11January1968 correction is therefore a genuine comparison with the published text. Stacks31.24 (01X1) gives the restriction-stable definition;31.25.2 (0EMG) proves the familiar stalk and affine-section identifications for locally noetherian schemes. EGA21.2.9 p261 was compared separately: the correction distinguishes a local trivialization from multiplication by its meromorphic generator. The crossed21.2.12 insertion is not adopted.',references=['EGAIV4','StacksMeromorphic','Stacks0EMG'])
upd(51,present_check='For the unrestricted kernel question, A=k[t] semidirect direct_sum_{n>=1}k[t]/t^n and B=A_t gives a finitely presented algebra whose kernel is not finite. However, final printed EGAIV4,16.1.5–6 pp6–7, assumes f:Y→X is an immersion. Deligne’s Y=A1 disjoint union Spec(k)→A1 is not an immersion. It therefore does not refute the final published proposition or its finite-presentation corollary.',literature_fate='The17October1966 objection concerns a draft or a proof then under discussion. The1967 printed statement has the immersion hypothesis, under which16.1.6 reduces to the finite ideal of a finitely presented immersion. This exact publication comparison resolves the apparent conflict, while the separate unrestricted algebra-kernel question has the negative answer proved here.',classification='A',references=['EGAIV4'])
upd(52,modern_statement='Let M be an A-module with decreasing filtration M_p and M_0=M, and f a finite sequence. In EGAIV19.5.5, bijectivity of the canonical comparison from (gr(M) tensor A/(f))[T] to the associated graded of the mixed filtration implies that f is quasi-regular on gr(M), without a separation hypothesis. No noetherianity or finiteness of M is needed for this implication.',present_check='Printed EGAIV4 pp206–209 gives a⇒b⇒c without separation. The stronger hypothesis that all subquotients of each graded piece are (f)-adically separated is needed for c⇒a. Printedp207 footnote explicitly credits P.Deligne and says his proof is reproduced. The exact statement, filtration and proof structure have been read.',literature_fate='Exact later publication: EGAIV4,19.5.5,1967, pp206–209, with explicit Deligne credit. The dated1966 letter records its earlier communication; no separate circulation date for the enclosure is known.',classification='A',confidence='high',references=['EGAIV4'])
upd(55,present_check='After faithfully flat completion of B, use a flat noetherian local A-algebra C surjecting onto completedB. Both N tensor_A C and completedM are finite C-modules. Resolve completedM by finite free C-modules; Artin–Rees makes pro-I-adic completion exact on the resulting complex of finite C-modules, so pro-homology equals the completion pro-system of homology. Flat base change identifies this with the stated A-Tor. Faithful flatness of B→Bhat reflects the pro-zero kernels and cokernels. The argument is supplied in proof_notes.md and is stronger than equality of inverse limits.',literature_fate='The Artin–Rees pro-Tor mechanism is explicitly proved in Stacks15.28.3 (0911), citing Quillen’s Lemma9.9; modern Cohen factorization supplies the flat noetherian cover. The relative two-module statement is independently reconstructed here from those exact ingredients; it is not claimed to be literally the statement of0911.',references=['Stacks0911','Stacks07NN','Stacks09Q9','EGAIII'])
upd(56,present_check='For a local map A→Bhat, first complete A and choose a complete regular local ring R surjecting onto Ahat. Stacks07NN constructs a formally smooth R-algebra S mapping onto Bhat. Set C=Ahat tensor_R S (with the complete quotient topology), not S alone: C is complete noetherian, flat over Ahat and hence A, and maps onto Bhat. Stacks09Q9 is a modern complete factorization. To achieve minimal closed fibre, quotient by lifts of an independent linear system in the kernel of the map on closed-fibre cotangent spaces; regularity and the local flatness criterion preserve flatness. The Q_ni exponent remains an ink alarm.')
upd(61,present_check='Three exact checks are now complete. Printed0.16.1.9 p24 assumes noetherianity; its dimension equality extends without it because the faithful finite-module determinant trick makes B/Ann_B(M) integral over A/Ann_A(M), and integral extensions preserve dimension. Printed0.19.8.3 pp110–111 claims uniqueness among homomorphisms from a prime local ring: locality is essential, since every Z_(p) maps nonlocally to Q. Printed0.20.4.13(iv) p127 has the integral-closure hypothesis; localization gives Omega_B/A tensor Frac(B)=Omega_Frac(B)/Frac(A)=0 for an algebraic separable fraction extension, without integral closure. The0.20.4.6 sign is a convention requiring coherent propagation. The19.4.6–7 simplification proposals are not independently certified. EGAIII4.3.6 is tracked separately pending its exact primary statement.',literature_fate='The numbered EGAIV1 passages were opened and compared to the1966 letter; they confirm the scope of the locality and unnecessary-normality observations. The remaining proposed proof simplifications retain a bounded verification gap. No claim of a later published errata list or priority beyond the dated letter is made.',references=['EGAIV1','EGAIII'])
